This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**:
VS Insiders [12328.396]

**Steps to Reproduce**:
```cs
MyClass.M();

public class MyClass
{
    public static void M()
    {
    }

    public void M(int i)
    {
    }
}
```
Hover over `M` in `MyClass.M();`

**Expected Behavior**:
Hover shows info about `static void M()` without overloads

**Actual Behavior**:
Hover shows info about `static void M()` and has `(+1 overload)` part:
<img width="308" height="113" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b3b9382f-5166-4f01-879c-efbb7d7949ec" />
This is incorrect since only one overload of `M` is applicable in the given context. Signature help, for instance, correctly filters out instance overload and shows only the static one:
<img width="145" height="79" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/39116fbd-d783-48c8-b3ff-49b37c044b8e" />

There is a case like this in the BCL: `System.Runtime.ExceptionServices.ExceptionDispatchInfo` has both static and instance overloads of `Throw`. This is actually how I discovered the bug.

**Version Used**: .NET SDK 10.0.400, `dotnet watch`, Windows 11 x64

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. `encmin.csproj`:

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
```

2. `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

var tick = 0;
while (true)
{
	var vm = new ViewModel();
	Console.WriteLine($"tick {tick++}: model is {vm.Model.GetType().Name}, ViewModel(.ctor) takes {typeof(ViewModel).GetConstructors(System.Reflection.BindingFlags.NonPublic | System.Reflection.BindingFlags.Instance)[0].GetParameters()[0].ParameterType.Name}");
	GC.Collect();
	await Task.Delay(1000);
}

[CreateNewOnMetadataUpdate]
public class Model
{
	public string Name = "model";
	// EDIT: add fields here
}

public class ViewModel
{
	public readonly object Model;

	// Not edited: keeps creating the original Model after the update.
	public ViewModel() : this(new Model()) { }

	protected ViewModel(Model model)
	{
		Model = model;
		// EDIT: write the added fields here
	}
}
```

3. `dotnet watch run`
4. In one save, replace `// EDIT: add fields here` with
   `public object? Extra1, Extra2, Extra3, Extra4, Extra5, Extra6, Extra7, Extra8;`
   and `// EDIT: write the added fields here` with
   ```csharp
   model.Extra1 = model.Extra2 = model.Extra3 = model.Extra4 = new byte[64];
   model.Extra5 = model.Extra6 = model.Extra7 = model.Extra8 = new object();
   ```

**Expected Behavior**:

The update applies and the app keeps running, or the edit is reported as unsupported (rude edit / restart). The caller that was not edited should never run code of `Model#1` on an instance of `Model`.

**Actual Behavior**:

```
tick 2: model is Model, ViewModel(.ctor) takes Model
dotnet watch : C# and Razor changes applied in 582ms.
tick 3: model is Model, ViewModel(.ctor) takes Model#1
Fatal error.
Internal CLR error. (0x80131506)
dotnet watch : [encmin (net10.0)] Exited with error code -1073741819
```

`Model` is replaced by `Model#1`, as designed. `ViewModel(Model)` is edited, so it is emitted as an **update of the same MethodDef** (same token) whose signature now reads `ViewModel(Model#1)`. The public ctor is not edited, so its IL still does `newobj Model::.ctor` and passes an original `Model` to the updated ctor. The updated body then writes `Model#1`'s fields on that `Model` instance, past the end of the object: the heap is corrupted and the process dies with an access violation.

With the same field layout nothing crashes, but `Model#1` code still runs with `this` being an instance of `Model` (observed: methods of `Model#1` called on the original instance through a closure of the updated ctor). The display class of the updated ctor is also reused, with its captured field still declared as `Model` while the updated IL stores a `Model#1` in it.

Note: this is not #70604 (unchanged call sites keep using the original type, which is by design). Here it's the **updated** method that is retyped in place while its unchanged callers still use the original type.

Possible expected handling: treat the signature change caused by a replaced type like any other signature change (emit a new method and leave the old one untouched, or report a rude edit), rather than updating the existing MethodDef with a new signature.

**Context**: Uno Platform's MVUX generates a `[CreateNewOnMetadataUpdate]` model partial and a bindable view model whose protected ctor takes the model. Any edit that changes that ctor's body (for example a command's parameters) hits this path: https://github.com/unoplatform/uno.extensions/issues/3295.

**Version Used**:

- Isolated reproduction: locally built `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Features`, product version `5.10.0-dev`, targeting `net10.0`, running on Windows with .NET 10.0.14.
- Source checkout inspected: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/tree/9d72f9c83c919963c7e54135fb0a6469c99ec9a6. This is the inspected checkout revision, not a verified source SHA for the prebuilt binary used in the test.
- Related end-to-end report: dotnet/maui#36157, using `dotnet watch run -f net11.0-maccatalyst`, CoreCLR, MAUI XAML source generation and incremental XAML Hot Reload, package `11.0.0-ci.pr34338.6.26312.9`.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

The Roslyn behavior below reproduces without MAUI or XAML language services. An incremental generator reads an additional file, reports an error when the text lacks a closing `}`, and always generates valid C#.

1. Create a workspace project with the generator and an additional file containing `{initial}`.
2. Create successive immutable project snapshots with additional-file contents `{broken`, `{repaired}`, and `{another valid edit}`.
3. Query each snapshot's source-generator diagnostics. The results are correctly `0, 1, 0, 0` errors.
4. Call `EditSession.GetProjectDifferencesAsync` with the broken snapshot as `oldProject` and the repaired snapshot as `newProject`.
5. Inspect the diagnostic collection returned by that call. It contains the old parse error even though `newProject` has no generator diagnostics.
6. Compare against clean-snapshot controls, as shown below.

The following standalone harness was run against existing Roslyn test-output binaries. Reflection only invokes the internal APIs under test; it does not modify private state. Compile against the matching framework reference assemblies plus `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.dll`, `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.dll`, `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Workspaces.dll`, `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces.dll`, and `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Features.dll`. Run as a .NET 10 application, passing the absolute path to the populated `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Features.UnitTests` output directory so its dependencies can be resolved.

