**Diagnostic Id**:

`MAUIG1003: Expression must end with '}'.`

The standalone generator deliberately uses the same ID/message as the MAUI report; it has no dependency on MAUI and no application-level cache.

**Expected Behavior**:

Generator errors returned for the current Hot Reload update should describe the new project snapshot. An error present only in `oldProject` should not be returned as a current error after `newProject` has repaired the input.

**Actual Behavior**:

The generator diagnostics themselves recover correctly:

```text
Generator baseline: 0 diagnostic(s)
Generator broken: 1 diagnostic(s)
Generator repaired: 0 diagnostic(s)
Generator another: 0 diagnostic(s)
```

EnC snapshot comparison reintroduces the old error:

```text
EnC baseline -> broken: 1 diagnostic(s): MAUIG1003
EnC broken -> repaired: 1 diagnostic(s): MAUIG1003
EnC baseline -> repaired: 0 diagnostic(s):
EnC broken -> another: 1 diagnostic(s): MAUIG1003
EnC repaired -> another: 0 diagnostic(s):
```

**Relationship to the MAUI report / scope**:

- dotnet/maui#36157 reports that valid edits apply to the running app while a previously reported `MAUIG1003` continues to appear under `dotnet watch`.
- A separate test with the real MAUI `XamlGenerator`, XIHR enabled, and the same generator driver retained across valid/broken/repaired/further-valid edits produced generator-error counts `0, 1, 0, 0`, with zero C# compilation errors at every stage. That test used newer local MAUI binaries (`11.0.0-dev`, built July 24, 2026), not the exact package in the original report.
- This issue tracks the independently reproduced Roslyn diagnostic-forwarding behavior. The exact macOS `dotnet watch` session was not reproduced, and it has not been established why a broken old snapshot would remain relevant across all of the repeated saves described in the MAUI report. That end-to-end connection still needs validation.

LSP go-to-definition and go-to-type-definition can return an old source offset after another open document changes. The offset is converted using the current document text, so the resulting line/column points at the wrong characters.

Reproduced on current main `ff2e51158d0f18bf40381246097f2a5dbd83118e`, with a locally built standalone server, and on NuGet `roslyn-language-server` 5.13.0-1.26514.10 (macOS arm64).

Minimal case:

```csharp
// A.cs
class A { public static void M() { } }
// B.cs
class B { void Use() { A.M(); } }
```

Open both documents, request the definition of `M` in `B.cs`, insert a comment line at the start of `A.cs` with `textDocument/didChange`, and request the definition from unchanged `B.cs` again. The result should identify `M` at its new position. Instead it can retain the old offset. A fresh server on the edited files returns the correct location. Inserting spaces on the same line also reproduces the problem.

A regression in `GoToDefinitionTests` reproduces all eight combinations of definition/type-definition, line/column movement, and mutating/non-mutating LSP workspaces. It is necessary to set `IWorkspacePartialSolutionsTestHook.IsPartialSolutionDisabled = false`: production enables partial semantics, but test workspaces disable them by default. All eight cases fail without a fix.

The likely cause is `AbstractNavigableItemsService` accepting a symbol from a frozen snapshot whose target tree is old, while the LSP handler resolves its `NavigableDocument` ID in the current solution. The optimization from #72714 is valuable; a proposed narrow fix preserves it when source locations use current trees and retries full semantics only for stale targets.

A separate [21-site probe](https://github.com/julymetodiev/lantern-lattice/pull/2) reproduces 12 wrong target columns after declaration movement on current main. This affects a compiler-backed call graph: caching these locations can link the wrong symbols. The probe itself is client-side diagnostic evidence, separate from the failing Roslyn regression.

