Looking at IL for the scenarios, it looks like the receiver is getting dereferenced before ```GetVal``` is evaluated

**Version used:** Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 5.9.0, on .NET 10

**Steps to reproduce:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Syntax;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.FlowAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Operations;

const string Source = """
    using System.Threading;
    class C
    {
        readonly Lock _gate = new();
        void Run()
        {
            lock (_gate)
            {
                System.GC.Collect();
            }
        }
    }
    """;

var references = ((string)AppContext.GetData("TRUSTED_PLATFORM_ASSEMBLIES")!)
    .Split(Path.PathSeparator)
    .Select(path => MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(path))
    .ToArray();

foreach (var nullable in new[] { NullableContextOptions.Disable, NullableContextOptions.Enable })
{
    var tree = CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(Source);
    var compilation = CSharpCompilation.Create(
        "repro",
        [tree],
        references,
        new CSharpCompilationOptions(OutputKind.DynamicallyLinkedLibrary, nullableContextOptions: nullable));
    var model = compilation.GetSemanticModel(tree);
    var method = tree.GetRoot().DescendantNodes().OfType<MethodDeclarationSyntax>().Single();
    var graph = ControlFlowGraph.Create(method, model)!;
    var lockStatement = method.DescendantNodes().OfType<LockStatementSyntax>().Single();
    var lockType = ((ILockOperation)model.GetOperation(lockStatement)!).LockedValue.Type!;
    var scopeOwner = lockType.GetMembers("EnterScope").OfType<IMethodSymbol>().Single().ReturnType.ContainingType;

    Console.WriteLine($"nullable: {nullable}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  compile errors: {compilation.GetDiagnostics().Count(d => d.Severity == DiagnosticSeverity.Error)}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  locked value annotation: {lockType.NullableAnnotation}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  equal to Scope's containing type, including nullability: {lockType.Equals(scopeOwner, SymbolEqualityComparer.IncludeNullability)}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  blocks: {graph.Blocks.Length}");
    Console.WriteLine($"  has finally region: {graph.Blocks.Any(b => b.EnclosingRegion.Kind == ControlFlowRegionKind.Finally)}");
}
```

**Diagnostic Id:** none. The code compiles and emits with no errors in both cases.

**Expected Behavior:**

The control-flow graph of a `lock` on a `System.Threading.Lock` has the same shape whatever the nullable context. It is lowered like `using (l.EnterScope()) { }`, with a `finally` region that disposes the scope.

**Actual Behavior:**

With nullable annotations enabled, the graph has no `EnterScope` call, no `finally` region and no `Dispose` call. The output is:

```
nullable: Disable
  compile errors: 0
  locked value annotation: None
  equal to Scope's containing type, including nullability: True
  blocks: 5
  has finally region: True
nullable: Enable
  compile errors: 0
  locked value annotation: NotAnnotated
  equal to Scope's containing type, including nullability: False
  blocks: 3
  has finally region: False
```

Only the control-flow graph is affected. Emit succeeds in both cases. Because nullable is on in most modern projects, an analyzer using this API sees no lock in the common case. The results also change within one project at a `#nullable disable` line.

**Likely cause:**

[`TryFindLockTypeInfo`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/19028adb353e231cbe4afbb63f9d6f3110f3e95d/src/Compilers/Core/Portable/Symbols/ISymbolExtensions.cs#L153) checks the scope type with:

```csharp
!lockType.Equals(scopeType.ContainingType, SymbolEqualityComparer.ConsiderEverything)
```

Here `lockType` is the locked value's type, annotated `NotAnnotated` under `#nullable enable`, while `scopeType.ContainingType` has annotation `None`. The comparison fails, the helper returns `null`, and `ControlFlowGraphBuilder.VisitLock` takes its fallback path, which visits the locked value and the body with no lowering.

Comparing with `SymbolEqualityComparer.Default` returns true in both cases in the repro above, so that may be the fix. I measured the comparison but have not built Roslyn with that change.
